Also known as fibrous histiocytoma or sclerosing haemangioma.  Also known as fibrous histiocytoma or sclerosing haemangioma.  The localized proliferation of fibroblasts is probably triggered by minor trauma such as an insect bite. These benign growths are common in young women. If you can make a confident diagnosis, no treatment is required—excision will merely leave another scar. Multiple dermatofibromas have been reported in conditions such as HIV infection, SLE, and other diseases in which the immune state is altered.  The localized proliferation of fibroblasts is probably triggered by minor trauma such as an insect bite. These benign growths are common in young women. If you can make a confident diagnosis, no treatment is required—excision will merely leave another scar. Multiple dermatofibromas have been reported in conditions such as HIV infection, SLE, and other diseases in which the immune state is altered.  What should I look for?  What should I look for?  •    A brownish red dermal nodule, $0.5{-}1mathsf{c m}$ in diameter, usually on the arm, shoulder, thigh, or leg. •    A brownish red dermal nodule, $0.5{-}1\mathsf{c m}$ in diameter, usually on the arm, shoulder, thigh, or leg.

[Handbook: Solar lentigo]
•    Solar lentigines: persistent flat, brown spots (liver spots) on exposed skin, e.g. backs of hands, forearms, face. Easy bruising (senile purpura) which resolves, leaving white stellate pseudoscars—​most obvious on forearms and backs of hands. Elastic tissue damaged by chronic sun exposure provides the small cutaneous blood vessels with inadequate support. Waxy coarse, yellowish skin (solar elastosis) on the forehead, temples, cheeks, and back of the neck, associated with deep furrows. •    Solar lentigines: persistent flat, brown spots (liver spots) on exposed skin, e.g. backs of hands, forearms, face. Easy bruising (senile purpura) which resolves, leaving white stellate pseudoscars—​most obvious on forearms and backs of hands. Elastic tissue damaged by chronic sun exposure provides the small cutaneous blood vessels with inadequate support. Waxy coarse, yellowish skin (solar elastosis) on the forehead, temples, cheeks, and back of the neck, associated with deep furrows.
What is a lentigo? (plural: lentigines or lentigos)  What is a lentigo? (plural: lentigines or lentigos)  Lentigines are small $cdot1{-}3mathrm{mm}$ in diameter), well-circumscribed, flat, brown to black ‘freckles’ that may appear on any body site. Lentigines do not fade when the skin is no longer exposed to sun, and are not necessarily triggered by sun exposure.  Lentigines are small $\cdot1{-}3\mathrm{mm}$ in diameter), well-circumscribed, flat, brown to black ‘freckles’ that may appear on any body site. Lentigines do not fade when the skin is no longer exposed to sun, and are not necessarily triggered by sun exposure.  Some normal individuals have numerous lentigines, but, in a child with ‘freckles’, you should consider:  Some normal individuals have numerous lentigines, but, in a child with ‘freckles’, you should consider:  · Neurofibromatosis 1—a RASopathy: freckling in skinfolds (see E p. 552). Incidence around 1 in 3000. · Neurofibromatosis 1—a RASopathy: freckling in skinfolds (see E p. 552). Incidence around 1 in 3000. This list is not exhaustive, and other rare syndromes are associated with multiple lentigines, either in a generalized or localized distribution.  This list is not exhaustive, and other rare syndromes are associated with multiple lentigines, either in a generalized or localized distribution.


